/**
 * A thousand ordinary sentences, two for every word in `common-words.js`.
 *
 * The reading deck has real nineteenth-century prose and the phrasebook has
 * the things you say in a shop. This is the middle: plain sentences of the
 * kind that make up most of a day, chosen so that the five hundred words
 * worth knowing each turn up twice, in two different shapes.
 *
 * Two sentences rather than one because a word met once is a word you have
 * seen, and a word met in two different sentences is a word you are starting
 * to own — *je pense à toi* and *qu'est-ce que tu en penses ?* are the same
 * verb doing different work.
 *
 * Written for this app. Short on purpose: a sentence you can hold in your
 * head is a sentence you can answer before the bus comes.
 *
 * Each row is one word and its two sentences, which is what keeps the
 * coverage honest — a word cannot quietly lose its examples.
 */

